WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:05.090 --> 00:00:07.790
DAN CIRULI: Hi, and welcome
to the Anthos Deep Dive

00:00:07.790 --> 00:00:09.380
Session, Part 2.

00:00:09.380 --> 00:00:12.110
My name is Dan Ciruli,
and I'm a product manager

00:00:12.110 --> 00:00:14.057
at Google Cloud.

00:00:14.057 --> 00:00:15.640
TONY PUJALS: And,
hi, I'm Tony Pujals.

00:00:15.640 --> 00:00:17.985
I'm a developer advocate
at Google Cloud.

00:00:17.985 --> 00:00:19.610
DAN CIRULI: And we're
going to walk you

00:00:19.610 --> 00:00:23.450
through the second part of
the Deep Dive on Anthos.

00:00:23.450 --> 00:00:26.270
I'm going to start with a little
bit of a level set to make sure

00:00:26.270 --> 00:00:29.180
that you're clear on what
the different components are

00:00:29.180 --> 00:00:31.280
within Anthos and
why they exist.

00:00:31.280 --> 00:00:34.710
And then I'm going to spend
some time deep diving on Anthos

00:00:34.710 --> 00:00:35.810
Service Mesh.

00:00:35.810 --> 00:00:40.290
And Tony is going to spend some
time on Cloud Run for Anthos.

00:00:40.290 --> 00:00:43.070
But first Anthos itself.

00:00:43.070 --> 00:00:47.030
Anthos is an application
modernization platform.

00:00:47.030 --> 00:00:50.510
It's a platform that lets you
write applications and run them

00:00:50.510 --> 00:00:51.800
anywhere--

00:00:51.800 --> 00:00:55.010
on Google Cloud, in
your own premises,

00:00:55.010 --> 00:00:57.650
or on other clouds--
even at the edge,

00:00:57.650 --> 00:01:01.520
in places like retail
facilities or warehouses.

00:01:01.520 --> 00:01:03.230
It's a software-based
solution that

00:01:03.230 --> 00:01:06.110
is aimed at solving the
problem of how difficult it

00:01:06.110 --> 00:01:09.170
is to run things in
different places right now.

00:01:09.170 --> 00:01:12.170
It's there to give
you consistency

00:01:12.170 --> 00:01:13.850
across the different
locations you're

00:01:13.850 --> 00:01:17.150
going to have to deploy in
order to make your developers

00:01:17.150 --> 00:01:21.890
and your operators more
efficient at their jobs.

00:01:21.890 --> 00:01:24.830
One of the key things that
Anthos enables you to do

00:01:24.830 --> 00:01:27.260
is to modernize in place.

00:01:27.260 --> 00:01:29.540
Moving to the cloud can be hard.

00:01:29.540 --> 00:01:32.450
Moving from old, monolithic
architectures to new ones

00:01:32.450 --> 00:01:33.320
can be hard.

00:01:33.320 --> 00:01:36.350
And if you try to do both of
those things at the same time,

00:01:36.350 --> 00:01:37.970
well, that's doubly hard.

00:01:37.970 --> 00:01:40.790
With Anthos you can modernize
your application where you

00:01:40.790 --> 00:01:43.520
are-- still in your data center,
if that's where you are--

00:01:43.520 --> 00:01:46.460
and move to the cloud
later as a second step.

00:01:46.460 --> 00:01:49.100
It's a much easier
process to follow.

00:01:49.100 --> 00:01:50.960
When you do start
running applications

00:01:50.960 --> 00:01:55.160
in multiple locations,
Anthos gives you consistency.

00:01:55.160 --> 00:01:58.700
It lets you do things like
write policy about security

00:01:58.700 --> 00:02:00.620
and have that
policy be in effect

00:02:00.620 --> 00:02:03.080
regardless of where the
application is running.

00:02:03.080 --> 00:02:07.040
This gives your developers, your
operators, your security people

00:02:07.040 --> 00:02:10.572
all consistency to run the
application the same way

00:02:10.572 --> 00:02:11.780
no matter where it's running.

00:02:14.300 --> 00:02:17.030
One key factor for
Anthos is that it is

00:02:17.030 --> 00:02:19.673
a 100% software-based solution.

00:02:19.673 --> 00:02:21.840
You don't need to invest
in new hardware for Anthos.

00:02:21.840 --> 00:02:24.260
It runs on your
existing hardware.

00:02:24.260 --> 00:02:29.390
And at its core, it's based on
a trio of open source projects.

00:02:29.390 --> 00:02:33.830
We do container management
using Anthos GKE, which

00:02:33.830 --> 00:02:37.310
is, of course, based on the
popular Kubernetes container

00:02:37.310 --> 00:02:38.810
deployment system.

00:02:38.810 --> 00:02:41.450
The service mesh component
is Anthos Service Mesh,

00:02:41.450 --> 00:02:45.410
which is based on Istio, the
popular open source service

00:02:45.410 --> 00:02:45.920
mesh.

00:02:45.920 --> 00:02:49.790
And up at the top giving your
developers a great development

00:02:49.790 --> 00:02:52.700
experience is Cloud
Run for Anthos.

00:02:52.700 --> 00:02:55.790
We also have a great
marketplace for the other things

00:02:55.790 --> 00:02:58.420
that you might want to run--
other software, databases,

00:02:58.420 --> 00:02:59.683
et cetera.

00:02:59.683 --> 00:03:01.100
And around this
whole thing, we've

00:03:01.100 --> 00:03:02.780
got a very good
policy management

00:03:02.780 --> 00:03:04.730
system that lets
you manage policy

00:03:04.730 --> 00:03:07.790
across all the different
locations and good operations

00:03:07.790 --> 00:03:11.330
tools, so that the people
who are running the software

00:03:11.330 --> 00:03:13.010
have the same
experience regardless

00:03:13.010 --> 00:03:14.060
of where it's running--

00:03:14.060 --> 00:03:17.690
on Google Cloud, in your own
premises, on other clouds,

00:03:17.690 --> 00:03:20.450
or at the edge.

00:03:20.450 --> 00:03:23.540
Now let me talk a little bit
about my favorite component

00:03:23.540 --> 00:03:27.890
in Anthos, Anthos Service Mesh.

00:03:27.890 --> 00:03:29.580
And to talk about
Anthos Service Mesh,

00:03:29.580 --> 00:03:33.040
I want to talk about a trend
in our industry of moving away

00:03:33.040 --> 00:03:35.350
from monolithic applications.

00:03:35.350 --> 00:03:38.020
Why did we decide to
move away from monoliths?

00:03:38.020 --> 00:03:40.840
Well, actually that second
bullet, to me, is the big one.

00:03:40.840 --> 00:03:42.940
Monoliths make it
slow to innovate.

00:03:42.940 --> 00:03:46.210
When you have 100 developers
working on an application,

00:03:46.210 --> 00:03:49.000
you're probably deploying
that application maybe

00:03:49.000 --> 00:03:52.460
once a quarter, maybe
once every six months.

00:03:52.460 --> 00:03:56.260
It's very difficult
to test and release

00:03:56.260 --> 00:03:59.290
when you're deploying
huge applications.

00:03:59.290 --> 00:04:00.880
They're also very
expensive to scale

00:04:00.880 --> 00:04:03.280
because monoliths tend
to run on specialized,

00:04:03.280 --> 00:04:04.660
very expensive hardware.

00:04:04.660 --> 00:04:08.600
And buying multiple instances
of that can be very expensive.

00:04:08.600 --> 00:04:14.060
An operation is typically
a very difficult process.

00:04:14.060 --> 00:04:16.390
The move to microservices
really addresses

00:04:16.390 --> 00:04:17.899
a lot of these
problems, and again,

00:04:17.899 --> 00:04:19.630
starting with innovation.

00:04:19.630 --> 00:04:21.910
With microservices,
you have small teams

00:04:21.910 --> 00:04:25.300
of developers who are each
deploying small units of code.

00:04:25.300 --> 00:04:27.280
Because they can
deploy independently,

00:04:27.280 --> 00:04:28.810
they can ship a lot faster.

00:04:28.810 --> 00:04:31.570
They can ship features
much, much more frequently.

00:04:31.570 --> 00:04:33.640
That means you end
up shipping features

00:04:33.640 --> 00:04:36.110
to your customers
a lot more often.

00:04:36.110 --> 00:04:37.840
And you can scale them
all independently.

00:04:37.840 --> 00:04:40.690
Microservices are typically
deployed in containers.

00:04:40.690 --> 00:04:42.610
They're running on
commodity hardware.

00:04:42.610 --> 00:04:44.530
And they scale independently.

00:04:44.530 --> 00:04:47.380
And finally, you've got a
great ecosystem of tools

00:04:47.380 --> 00:04:50.990
around operations that
make operating these easy.

00:04:50.990 --> 00:04:52.930
So what is a service mesh?

00:04:52.930 --> 00:04:55.505
Well, a service
mesh helps you solve

00:04:55.505 --> 00:04:57.130
some of those issues
you'll have as you

00:04:57.130 --> 00:04:59.020
decompose your application.

00:04:59.020 --> 00:05:01.480
A service mesh
transparently, and in

00:05:01.480 --> 00:05:06.430
a language-independent way, adds
functionality into your network

00:05:06.430 --> 00:05:09.610
calls so that your
developers don't

00:05:09.610 --> 00:05:14.110
have to write a bunch of new
infrastructure to handle those.

00:05:14.110 --> 00:05:17.830
It's a core component of Anthos,
we offer Anthos Service Mesh.

00:05:17.830 --> 00:05:20.590
As I said before, this
is based on Istio,

00:05:20.590 --> 00:05:24.880
the most popular service mesh
application in open source

00:05:24.880 --> 00:05:25.580
today.

00:05:25.580 --> 00:05:27.160
And what does it
do specifically?

00:05:27.160 --> 00:05:28.870
Well, there's three
main things that you

00:05:28.870 --> 00:05:30.250
get from a service mesh.

00:05:30.250 --> 00:05:33.550
First, it gives you the ability
to understand the traffic

00:05:33.550 --> 00:05:34.880
between your services.

00:05:34.880 --> 00:05:37.690
Now that your monolith is
broken up into microservices,

00:05:37.690 --> 00:05:41.350
you've got a lot of components
communicating with each other.

00:05:41.350 --> 00:05:45.950
And a service mesh can make
sure that you are monitoring,

00:05:45.950 --> 00:05:48.170
logging, and tracing
all of that traffic

00:05:48.170 --> 00:05:50.360
so you can understand
your application.

00:05:50.360 --> 00:05:53.410
Second, a service mesh
gives you advance networking

00:05:53.410 --> 00:05:57.070
functionality to make it so you
can have operational agility,

00:05:57.070 --> 00:06:00.430
make it so you can safely
roll out new components,

00:06:00.430 --> 00:06:02.710
do locality-base load
balancing if you're

00:06:02.710 --> 00:06:04.520
running in multiple clusters.

00:06:04.520 --> 00:06:07.930
And finally, a service mesh lets
you secure all of that traffic

00:06:07.930 --> 00:06:10.360
and ensure that everything
is encrypted in transit

00:06:10.360 --> 00:06:13.090
and you can write policy
to dictate who is allowed

00:06:13.090 --> 00:06:15.430
to communicate with whom.

00:06:15.430 --> 00:06:18.940
Today, I'm going to focus on two
key areas here in understanding

00:06:18.940 --> 00:06:21.640
your application and
the ability to use

00:06:21.640 --> 00:06:25.120
some of these advanced
networking capabilities.

00:06:25.120 --> 00:06:29.140
And to do that, we're going
to use some chaos engineering

00:06:29.140 --> 00:06:32.410
and inject a little chaos
into our application.

00:06:32.410 --> 00:06:35.770
Chaos engineering lets
you test the resiliency

00:06:35.770 --> 00:06:38.830
of your application by
seeing what happens when

00:06:38.830 --> 00:06:40.980
different components break.

00:06:40.980 --> 00:06:42.820
Anthos Service
Mesh, in addition to

00:06:42.820 --> 00:06:47.068
a lot of other things it can do,
can allow you to inject chaos.

00:06:47.068 --> 00:06:48.610
And what I'm going
to demonstrate now

00:06:48.610 --> 00:06:50.650
is how we can inject
some chaos to see

00:06:50.650 --> 00:06:53.980
how our application behaves
if one of the components

00:06:53.980 --> 00:06:56.860
starts running more slowly
or if one of the components

00:06:56.860 --> 00:06:59.210
starts failing.

00:06:59.210 --> 00:07:01.520
For this demonstration,
I'll be using

00:07:01.520 --> 00:07:03.890
an e-commerce application.

00:07:03.890 --> 00:07:06.430
It was developed
using microservices

00:07:06.430 --> 00:07:11.130
so we can deploy frequently
and independently.

00:07:11.130 --> 00:07:11.630
As

00:07:11.630 --> 00:07:14.780
You can see, it's a
nice modern application

00:07:14.780 --> 00:07:18.310
with a bunch of
services underneath.

00:07:18.310 --> 00:07:20.940
Let's look and see what
Anthos Service Mesh can

00:07:20.940 --> 00:07:23.310
do to help us understand
this application

00:07:23.310 --> 00:07:26.100
and how failures might
contribute to it.

00:07:28.770 --> 00:07:31.890
A service mesh is gathering
all kinds of information

00:07:31.890 --> 00:07:35.430
about our application, not
just the telemetry that's

00:07:35.430 --> 00:07:39.840
being gathered but the actual
topology of the application--

00:07:39.840 --> 00:07:45.510
which services are communicating
with which other services.

00:07:45.510 --> 00:07:47.790
I can see every service
in my application

00:07:47.790 --> 00:07:51.000
as well as how they relate
to every others, including

00:07:51.000 --> 00:07:53.230
how much traffic they're
sending to others.

00:07:53.230 --> 00:07:55.500
This is some
powerful information.

00:07:55.500 --> 00:07:58.710
And for any of these
services, I can dive in,

00:07:58.710 --> 00:08:02.370
and I can understand
them even more deeply.

00:08:02.370 --> 00:08:04.170
Here I'm getting
tons of information

00:08:04.170 --> 00:08:06.430
about my front-end application.

00:08:06.430 --> 00:08:08.430
This is the one that
users actually hit,

00:08:08.430 --> 00:08:12.010
so it's the one I care
the most about it.

00:08:12.010 --> 00:08:15.120
As with every other service
that's deployed in the mesh,

00:08:15.120 --> 00:08:17.460
I get great telemetry on it.

00:08:17.460 --> 00:08:20.730
I can see how much
traffic this is serving.

00:08:20.730 --> 00:08:22.950
I can see things like
what its error rates are

00:08:22.950 --> 00:08:25.170
and how they change
over time, as well

00:08:25.170 --> 00:08:27.600
as what its latency are.

00:08:27.600 --> 00:08:31.466
I can do this on any
one of many time spans.

00:08:31.466 --> 00:08:33.299
I can zoom out a little
bit and see how it's

00:08:33.299 --> 00:08:36.010
been performing during the day.

00:08:36.010 --> 00:08:38.520
One of the great features
about Anthos Service Mesh

00:08:38.520 --> 00:08:42.130
is the ability to set
service-level objectives.

00:08:42.130 --> 00:08:44.830
As I said, this is
really important,

00:08:44.830 --> 00:08:46.350
especially on the
front end for me

00:08:46.350 --> 00:08:48.940
to assess the health
of my application.

00:08:48.940 --> 00:08:51.740
I can see that this application
is currently healthy.

00:08:51.740 --> 00:08:53.910
And I've got an
objective here that says

00:08:53.910 --> 00:08:56.250
a certain percentage--

00:08:56.250 --> 00:09:00.660
95% of my requests
should be fulfilled

00:09:00.660 --> 00:09:02.790
within 200 milliseconds.

00:09:02.790 --> 00:09:04.800
However, earlier
today, you can see

00:09:04.800 --> 00:09:08.420
where I injected a little
chaos in the system.

00:09:08.420 --> 00:09:12.060
And I'm going to show
you how I did that.

00:09:12.060 --> 00:09:16.980
I use Anthos Service Mesh
and its use of the Istio APIs

00:09:16.980 --> 00:09:20.430
to inject a delay in my service.

00:09:20.430 --> 00:09:22.450
I've got a recommendation
service in here.

00:09:22.450 --> 00:09:25.660
It recommends new
products to my customers.

00:09:25.660 --> 00:09:27.960
And I said 10% of
the time, I want

00:09:27.960 --> 00:09:33.180
to add one-second delay to
see what the effect of that

00:09:33.180 --> 00:09:34.860
would be on the system.

00:09:34.860 --> 00:09:36.960
The system seems to
behave pretty well,

00:09:36.960 --> 00:09:41.820
but let's look closely again at
that service-level objective.

00:09:41.820 --> 00:09:46.880
I can see that I started failing
that objective some of the time

00:09:46.880 --> 00:09:49.310
when I injected that chaos.

00:09:49.310 --> 00:09:52.220
And if I look at my error
budget over this time frame,

00:09:52.220 --> 00:09:55.740
I can actually see it's going
down relatively rapidly.

00:09:55.740 --> 00:09:57.780
Now the system isn't
telling me that I'm

00:09:57.780 --> 00:09:59.207
going to run out today.

00:09:59.207 --> 00:10:01.540
In fact, it thinks I'm going
to make it through the day.

00:10:01.540 --> 00:10:04.560
But this does tell me, as the
person running this service,

00:10:04.560 --> 00:10:07.230
I've got a dependency
that I'm really--

00:10:07.230 --> 00:10:08.813
that is very critical for me.

00:10:08.813 --> 00:10:10.980
If the recommendation service
were to actually start

00:10:10.980 --> 00:10:13.740
performing like this, I would
be close to-- and maybe even

00:10:13.740 --> 00:10:14.610
would--

00:10:14.610 --> 00:10:18.280
exceed my SLO and run
out of error budget.

00:10:18.280 --> 00:10:19.960
So what am I going to do?

00:10:19.960 --> 00:10:22.560
Well, first I would tell
the recommendations team,

00:10:22.560 --> 00:10:24.150
you should be setting
your own SLOs.

00:10:24.150 --> 00:10:26.980
I need you to be returning
faster than one second.

00:10:26.980 --> 00:10:29.790
So I want you to have a
stricter SLO than that

00:10:29.790 --> 00:10:31.350
so you catch your own errors.

00:10:31.350 --> 00:10:33.720
But second, it tells me
that I should make sure

00:10:33.720 --> 00:10:37.440
that the front end is
resilient and, in fact,

00:10:37.440 --> 00:10:40.110
maybe can time out if the
recommendation service takes

00:10:40.110 --> 00:10:40.950
too long.

00:10:40.950 --> 00:10:43.962
Anthos Service Mesh can
help with that as well.

00:10:43.962 --> 00:10:46.170
Now I'm going to talk about
a different sort of chaos

00:10:46.170 --> 00:10:49.410
we can inject and
that is failures.

00:10:49.410 --> 00:10:52.290
As we said before,
the service mesh

00:10:52.290 --> 00:10:58.330
is telling me exactly how
my system is related--

00:10:58.330 --> 00:11:00.730
how many services
comprise it and how

00:11:00.730 --> 00:11:02.560
they relate to each other.

00:11:02.560 --> 00:11:04.870
Here, you can see
my front end talks

00:11:04.870 --> 00:11:07.240
to a service called
the cart service.

00:11:07.240 --> 00:11:10.750
Now the cart service
is an important service

00:11:10.750 --> 00:11:11.740
in my application.

00:11:11.740 --> 00:11:14.680
People can't see what they're
buying if they can't put things

00:11:14.680 --> 00:11:15.460
in the cart.

00:11:15.460 --> 00:11:18.340
However, if that should
fail at some point,

00:11:18.340 --> 00:11:21.020
I still want the
system to be resilient.

00:11:21.020 --> 00:11:22.720
So I'm going to
use Anthos Service

00:11:22.720 --> 00:11:25.240
Mesh to inject some chaos.

00:11:25.240 --> 00:11:27.550
I've got a file here
called cart error.

00:11:27.550 --> 00:11:30.680
Again, it's using the
open source Istio APIs.

00:11:30.680 --> 00:11:32.140
And it's saying,
hey, in the cart

00:11:32.140 --> 00:11:37.897
service I want you 100% of
the time to throw a 500.

00:11:37.897 --> 00:11:39.980
I'm not going to have to
redeploy the application,

00:11:39.980 --> 00:11:42.147
and I'm not going to have
to recode the application.

00:11:42.147 --> 00:11:44.590
I'm just going to tell
the service mesh to start

00:11:44.590 --> 00:11:46.030
returning these errors.

00:11:46.030 --> 00:11:55.420
I've already made this change,
and I have made a commitment.

00:11:55.420 --> 00:11:58.570
And I can see all I
need to do is push this,

00:11:58.570 --> 00:12:01.150
and it will-- or
commit this, push it,

00:12:01.150 --> 00:12:02.990
and it will take effect.

00:12:02.990 --> 00:12:08.650
So we're got to inject
some chaos here.

00:12:12.250 --> 00:12:13.990
And then I'm going to push this.

00:12:13.990 --> 00:12:18.040
Now, my system of record, which
is the source code repository,

00:12:18.040 --> 00:12:19.690
has the new policy.

00:12:19.690 --> 00:12:22.750
Anthos Config Management
is taking that policy

00:12:22.750 --> 00:12:25.920
and pushing it into
each of my clusters.

00:12:25.920 --> 00:12:29.150
And as soon as they do, I can
see the effects of that policy.

00:12:29.150 --> 00:12:32.020
So my application is
still here and running.

00:12:32.020 --> 00:12:35.540
And wow, is that a
catastrophic failure.

00:12:35.540 --> 00:12:38.590
Now again, the shopping
cart service is important,

00:12:38.590 --> 00:12:40.460
but this is not acceptable.

00:12:40.460 --> 00:12:43.340
This is telling me that I
need to be more resilient.

00:12:43.340 --> 00:12:46.780
So now I'm going to have to go
to the front-end application

00:12:46.780 --> 00:12:53.110
team and make sure that they
are resilient to a failure

00:12:53.110 --> 00:12:55.980
from the shopping
cart application.

00:12:55.980 --> 00:13:00.150
Now I do want to say, you
saw that injecting chaos

00:13:00.150 --> 00:13:02.380
put some serious breakage
in our application.

00:13:02.380 --> 00:13:04.380
I don't recommend you do
this in your production

00:13:04.380 --> 00:13:06.480
in clusters, at least at first.

00:13:06.480 --> 00:13:08.380
Try this in your dev clusters.

00:13:08.380 --> 00:13:11.880
Now I'd like to turn
it over to Tony,

00:13:11.880 --> 00:13:14.940
who is going to talk about
Cloud Run for Anthos.

00:13:14.940 --> 00:13:16.770
TONY PUJALS: Thanks, Dan.

00:13:16.770 --> 00:13:19.080
So Dan, just did
a really great job

00:13:19.080 --> 00:13:21.840
of explaining the benefits
of microservice architecture

00:13:21.840 --> 00:13:26.310
and what Anthos does to help
you manage these applications

00:13:26.310 --> 00:13:27.540
that you've deployed.

00:13:27.540 --> 00:13:30.970
But what can we do to improve
that experience for developers?

00:13:30.970 --> 00:13:33.780
So the metaphor here is I want
to be productive in my space.

00:13:33.780 --> 00:13:34.940
I want enjoy my space.

00:13:34.940 --> 00:13:37.440
But I don't want have to do all
the work to create the space

00:13:37.440 --> 00:13:38.910
and maintain it.

00:13:38.910 --> 00:13:41.970
So how can Cloud Run for
Anthos help us with that?

00:13:41.970 --> 00:13:44.100
What we see here is that
Cloud Run for Anthos

00:13:44.100 --> 00:13:47.090
is right here at the
top of this stack,

00:13:47.090 --> 00:13:51.140
meaning that it is focused
on the developer experience.

00:13:51.140 --> 00:13:54.050
And what can we do to improve
that developer experience?

00:13:54.050 --> 00:13:55.820
We're going to get to that.

00:13:55.820 --> 00:13:57.770
Why would you choose
Cloud Run for Anthos?

00:13:57.770 --> 00:13:59.510
In a nutshell, the
idea is that you're

00:13:59.510 --> 00:14:02.360
going to get all the
benefits of containerized

00:14:02.360 --> 00:14:04.970
of your microservice
applications

00:14:04.970 --> 00:14:07.905
but without the overhead
of managing infrastructure

00:14:07.905 --> 00:14:09.530
and dealing with some
of the complexity

00:14:09.530 --> 00:14:13.910
that Kubernetes introduces
when you work at the Kubernetes

00:14:13.910 --> 00:14:16.140
level for your applications.

00:14:16.140 --> 00:14:18.650
So looking here, what
we see is a spectrum

00:14:18.650 --> 00:14:20.660
where you can
deploy applications

00:14:20.660 --> 00:14:24.650
directly on-prem as
vm.prem or in the cloud.

00:14:24.650 --> 00:14:27.620
And on the far right, we
have a fully managed service

00:14:27.620 --> 00:14:29.760
experience.

00:14:29.760 --> 00:14:32.160
But for whatever reason
that your org has chosen

00:14:32.160 --> 00:14:34.530
Anthos is likely
because you need

00:14:34.530 --> 00:14:38.070
to run certain applications that
don't fit the service model.

00:14:38.070 --> 00:14:40.600
You've got infrastructure
that you do have to manage.

00:14:40.600 --> 00:14:42.840
But for those applications
that could benefit

00:14:42.840 --> 00:14:45.900
from the serviceless
approach, you

00:14:45.900 --> 00:14:47.590
can leverage Cloud
Run for Anthos

00:14:47.590 --> 00:14:49.590
without having to worry
about the infrastructure

00:14:49.590 --> 00:14:51.990
for those applications
that you deploy.

00:14:51.990 --> 00:14:54.750
Cloud Run for Anthos
leverages is Knative, which

00:14:54.750 --> 00:14:57.270
is built on top of Kubernetes.

00:14:57.270 --> 00:14:59.667
And therefore, your
operators and everybody

00:14:59.667 --> 00:15:01.500
in your environment
familiar with Kubernetes

00:15:01.500 --> 00:15:04.900
already gets to leverage that.

00:15:04.900 --> 00:15:06.445
But from the
developer experience,

00:15:06.445 --> 00:15:08.820
you're going to be able to
deploy your applications using

00:15:08.820 --> 00:15:10.770
a much more simplified model.

00:15:10.770 --> 00:15:13.170
And I'm going to demonstrate
exactly what that means

00:15:13.170 --> 00:15:16.230
because I'm going to take
the same app that Dan was

00:15:16.230 --> 00:15:18.870
demonstrating, and I'm
going to migrate it

00:15:18.870 --> 00:15:21.600
to Cloud Run for Anthos.

00:15:21.600 --> 00:15:22.870
All right, let's dive in.

00:15:22.870 --> 00:15:24.780
This is the repo for
the microservices demo

00:15:24.780 --> 00:15:27.900
app that's launched as part of
the Anthos sample deployment

00:15:27.900 --> 00:15:29.400
that Dan used.

00:15:29.400 --> 00:15:32.040
It's a fairly
high-fidelity example

00:15:32.040 --> 00:15:35.970
of a sophisticated
microservices application built

00:15:35.970 --> 00:15:37.920
for a fictional enterprise.

00:15:37.920 --> 00:15:42.090
It's published as an e-commerce
website called Online Boutique.

00:15:42.090 --> 00:15:48.510
And architecturally, it's
composed of 10 microservices

00:15:48.510 --> 00:15:50.700
that communicate over GRPC.

00:15:50.700 --> 00:15:55.860
And it's got a data store that
we've simulated with Redis.

00:15:55.860 --> 00:15:58.170
And also we've added
a load generator

00:15:58.170 --> 00:16:01.800
so that we can simulate a
bunch of users sending traffic

00:16:01.800 --> 00:16:03.810
to the public front end.

00:16:03.810 --> 00:16:07.200
It's typically deployed
as a Kubernetes plus Istio

00:16:07.200 --> 00:16:09.960
application, as Dan
previously demonstrated.

00:16:09.960 --> 00:16:11.700
So what I'm going
to show you is how

00:16:11.700 --> 00:16:14.880
with just some
configuration changes,

00:16:14.880 --> 00:16:17.430
you can take advantage of a
simplified serviceless model

00:16:17.430 --> 00:16:21.900
and seamlessly integrate this
with existing workloads running

00:16:21.900 --> 00:16:23.550
on your Kubernetes clusters.

00:16:23.550 --> 00:16:26.350
And that's the main point I'm
going to be driving here today.

00:16:26.350 --> 00:16:28.650
So in other words,
this demo isn't

00:16:28.650 --> 00:16:31.920
going to be about all the
benefits of serverless per se.

00:16:31.920 --> 00:16:35.318
There's already a ton
of content about that.

00:16:35.318 --> 00:16:37.860
More importantly, I want to show
you how Cloud Run for Anthos

00:16:37.860 --> 00:16:40.650
makes it easy for your
ops team to provide you

00:16:40.650 --> 00:16:44.070
with the benefits of serviceless
coexisting seamlessly

00:16:44.070 --> 00:16:46.230
in your Kubernetes environments.

00:16:46.230 --> 00:16:48.490
This will leverage your
operator's expertise,

00:16:48.490 --> 00:16:50.430
along with the benefits
of using Anthos,

00:16:50.430 --> 00:16:53.280
our application
modernization platform.

00:16:53.280 --> 00:16:55.170
And so with Cloud
Run for Anthos,

00:16:55.170 --> 00:16:58.630
you're going to get to exploit
the best developer deployment

00:16:58.630 --> 00:17:01.110
model that makes the most
sense for each service

00:17:01.110 --> 00:17:04.380
that you build and deploy.

00:17:04.380 --> 00:17:05.794
And just to take a look at--

00:17:09.220 --> 00:17:13.200
here are the Kubernetes
manifests and the Istio

00:17:13.200 --> 00:17:15.720
manifests.

00:17:15.720 --> 00:17:21.140
And so what we're
going to do is--

00:17:21.140 --> 00:17:24.560
what you'll want to do
is fork my repo here,

00:17:24.560 --> 00:17:31.945
Cloud Run for Anthos Labs and
go to this sample right here.

00:17:31.945 --> 00:17:33.320
And here all the
instructions are

00:17:33.320 --> 00:17:35.750
provided, so everything
that I'm going to show you

00:17:35.750 --> 00:17:37.880
can be found in this
repo, including a script

00:17:37.880 --> 00:17:39.490
that you're going to need--

00:17:39.490 --> 00:17:42.080
well, that you can use for
creating a Kubernetes cluster

00:17:42.080 --> 00:17:42.680
with GKE.

00:17:48.180 --> 00:17:52.620
And if you want to create a
cluster using the web console,

00:17:52.620 --> 00:17:56.340
just remember to make sure that
you check the box Enable Cloud

00:17:56.340 --> 00:17:58.950
Run for Anthos for the cluster.

00:17:58.950 --> 00:18:00.900
This line here
basically is the part

00:18:00.900 --> 00:18:07.950
that ensures that our cluster
is going to have the full Cloud

00:18:07.950 --> 00:18:10.980
Run for Anthos support enabled.

00:18:10.980 --> 00:18:15.190
Now I have already done
this to save some time,

00:18:15.190 --> 00:18:25.800
so let's go to our Kubernetes
Engine dashboard and confirm.

00:18:25.800 --> 00:18:27.630
I've got cluster 1 here.

00:18:27.630 --> 00:18:34.350
This is just a Kubernetes
cluster running on GKE.

00:18:34.350 --> 00:18:37.500
And this is what was
installed with the cluster--

00:18:37.500 --> 00:18:39.960
with the Cloud Run
support enabled.

00:18:39.960 --> 00:18:42.395
The only services
running on it right now--

00:18:42.395 --> 00:18:44.710
let me sort here by namespace.

00:18:44.710 --> 00:18:46.660
So within the GKE
connect namespace,

00:18:46.660 --> 00:18:51.420
what we're going to see is
support for Cloud Run error

00:18:51.420 --> 00:18:56.940
reporting and logging to
Stackdriver, control plane

00:18:56.940 --> 00:19:01.020
component for configuring
our cluster local gateway

00:19:01.020 --> 00:19:04.320
for all the traffic and
microservice and microservice

00:19:04.320 --> 00:19:08.630
internal traffic, as well as our
public ingress to the cluster.

00:19:08.630 --> 00:19:14.070
And these last four
services are all

00:19:14.070 --> 00:19:16.440
required to provide that
serviceless foundation

00:19:16.440 --> 00:19:21.427
on top of Kubernetes that makes
Cloud Run for Anthos work.

00:19:21.427 --> 00:19:23.010
So now that we've
got a cluster, we're

00:19:23.010 --> 00:19:25.383
going to want to
deploy our application.

00:19:25.383 --> 00:19:27.300
Let's take a look at the
configuration changes

00:19:27.300 --> 00:19:31.140
that we need to make this work
with Cloud Run for Anthos.

00:19:31.140 --> 00:19:34.560
Quick refresher, here's the
architecture of our application

00:19:34.560 --> 00:19:35.680
once again.

00:19:35.680 --> 00:19:38.010
I want to point out that
the load generator is

00:19:38.010 --> 00:19:43.890
a long-running service that
is intended to send traffic

00:19:43.890 --> 00:19:45.420
as long as that
service is running

00:19:45.420 --> 00:19:47.620
and until it's terminated.

00:19:47.620 --> 00:19:49.140
So as a long-running
service, this

00:19:49.140 --> 00:19:55.680
is not a good candidate for
running as a serviceless app.

00:19:55.680 --> 00:19:57.120
And therefore,
this is going to be

00:19:57.120 --> 00:20:00.390
deployed as a standard
Kubernetes service.

00:20:00.390 --> 00:20:05.280
So the same thing is true
for Redis, which is also not

00:20:05.280 --> 00:20:09.420
a good candidate for serviceless
because it requires an attached

00:20:09.420 --> 00:20:11.100
volume.

00:20:11.100 --> 00:20:13.080
And this isn't
consistent with the goals

00:20:13.080 --> 00:20:18.540
of running as a Knative service
under the hood for auto scaling

00:20:18.540 --> 00:20:20.050
purposes.

00:20:20.050 --> 00:20:22.180
It's not a good fit for
the serviceless model.

00:20:22.180 --> 00:20:24.640
So this is a
long-running service,

00:20:24.640 --> 00:20:27.300
and this is a
state-full service.

00:20:27.300 --> 00:20:32.580
These are both going to be
deployed as normal Kubernetes

00:20:32.580 --> 00:20:34.050
deployments.

00:20:34.050 --> 00:20:39.690
The rest of these will be
deployed as serviceless apps.

00:20:39.690 --> 00:20:42.750
So what we're going to
look at is the delta

00:20:42.750 --> 00:20:47.580
between the original Kubernetes
manifest in this repo

00:20:47.580 --> 00:20:52.410
and the manifest
that I have added

00:20:52.410 --> 00:20:57.484
for you in this repo under
the Knative directory.

00:21:01.172 --> 00:21:02.630
And what I'm going
to do, actually,

00:21:02.630 --> 00:21:06.550
is I'm going to switch
over to my editor

00:21:06.550 --> 00:21:09.640
because I want to give you
a little bit of an overview

00:21:09.640 --> 00:21:12.762
about what changes
before I actually

00:21:12.762 --> 00:21:13.720
drill into the details.

00:21:16.400 --> 00:21:21.010
So for one thing, Knative offers
a simplified configuration

00:21:21.010 --> 00:21:21.680
abstraction.

00:21:21.680 --> 00:21:24.940
There's a single
service here compared

00:21:24.940 --> 00:21:29.250
to the need for two different
configurations-- a service

00:21:29.250 --> 00:21:32.350
and a deployment--
under Kubernetes.

00:21:32.350 --> 00:21:35.350
And because of
that, you don't need

00:21:35.350 --> 00:21:37.300
all of these
selectors and labels

00:21:37.300 --> 00:21:40.270
to correlate the
two configurations.

00:21:43.180 --> 00:21:45.930
You also don't need
to predetermined

00:21:45.930 --> 00:21:48.100
the number of
replicas you're going

00:21:48.100 --> 00:21:52.023
to need in anticipation of load
and availability requirements.

00:21:52.023 --> 00:21:53.440
You can take
advantage of the fact

00:21:53.440 --> 00:21:57.200
that Knative provides auto
scaling out of the box,

00:21:57.200 --> 00:22:00.460
you can still configure
minimum and maximum limits

00:22:00.460 --> 00:22:02.980
if you want to, but
again, you don't

00:22:02.980 --> 00:22:07.750
have to anticipate that load.

00:22:07.750 --> 00:22:11.810
And if you only have a single
container in your service,

00:22:11.810 --> 00:22:14.500
you don't even need to
give the container a name.

00:22:14.500 --> 00:22:16.480
Minor simplifications
but when you

00:22:16.480 --> 00:22:19.600
look at all of these in
aggregate, what you'll find

00:22:19.600 --> 00:22:24.700
is that Knative offers a very
nice simplified configuration

00:22:24.700 --> 00:22:29.870
abstraction for your
service resources.

00:22:29.870 --> 00:22:32.740
So now let's dive into
the actual configuration

00:22:32.740 --> 00:22:35.570
in more detail.

00:22:35.570 --> 00:22:38.110
Here, under the
Knative V1 directory,

00:22:38.110 --> 00:22:41.890
let's open up this
services YAML file.

00:22:41.890 --> 00:22:45.300
And actually, the first one
that I want to start with,

00:22:45.300 --> 00:22:47.915
I'll start with the
front end since that's

00:22:47.915 --> 00:22:52.960
the logical entry point
for the application anyway.

00:22:52.960 --> 00:22:54.502
What we see here--

00:22:54.502 --> 00:22:56.710
so the front-end services
is exposed to the internet.

00:22:56.710 --> 00:22:58.210
It serves the web
user interface,

00:22:58.210 --> 00:23:02.410
and it communicates with
various back-end services.

00:23:02.410 --> 00:23:06.640
What you'll see here,
obviously, the first two lines

00:23:06.640 --> 00:23:12.100
declaring that this is
a service resource using

00:23:12.100 --> 00:23:13.775
the Knative serving API.

00:23:17.110 --> 00:23:18.910
We've provided a
name and metadata.

00:23:18.910 --> 00:23:21.220
That name needs to be
unique within the namespace.

00:23:21.220 --> 00:23:23.150
There's no namespace
that we specified here,

00:23:23.150 --> 00:23:30.310
so the default is default.

00:23:30.310 --> 00:23:33.890
There's only one
container provided here.

00:23:33.890 --> 00:23:35.960
We only need one container
for this service.

00:23:35.960 --> 00:23:39.010
So there is no name for it.

00:23:39.010 --> 00:23:41.710
The front-end service
expects environment variables

00:23:41.710 --> 00:23:45.580
with DNS name colon
port values to be

00:23:45.580 --> 00:23:47.290
set for each of the
back-end services

00:23:47.290 --> 00:23:49.330
that it will send requests to.

00:23:49.330 --> 00:23:52.480
So note also that
all of these services

00:23:52.480 --> 00:23:55.030
are in the default namespace
as well as indicated

00:23:55.030 --> 00:23:58.030
by their DNS names.

00:23:58.030 --> 00:24:01.030
The Kubernetes compute
resource requirements

00:24:01.030 --> 00:24:08.260
for the container are specified
here and at least 10% of a VCU

00:24:08.260 --> 00:24:15.730
limited to 20% and at least
64 megabytes to start with

00:24:15.730 --> 00:24:18.840
and a limit of double that.

00:24:18.840 --> 00:24:24.790
Since the front-end service
listens on the port identified

00:24:24.790 --> 00:24:27.820
at runtime with the port
environment variable that

00:24:27.820 --> 00:24:30.130
is automatically set
by Knative, there's

00:24:30.130 --> 00:24:33.190
no need to specify a
container port value here

00:24:33.190 --> 00:24:35.200
as some of the
other services will

00:24:35.200 --> 00:24:36.920
that we'll see in a moment.

00:24:36.920 --> 00:24:39.100
The Default for
the port is 8080,

00:24:39.100 --> 00:24:41.080
but it's a good practice
for a Knative service

00:24:41.080 --> 00:24:44.000
to get the value of that
port environment variable

00:24:44.000 --> 00:24:45.760
anyway, which this app does.

00:24:50.000 --> 00:24:54.120
So now that we've covered
that, let's move on.

00:24:54.120 --> 00:24:57.200
Let's go to the Add Service.

00:24:57.200 --> 00:25:01.550
So the front end fetches
short text advertisements

00:25:01.550 --> 00:25:04.300
to display from Add
Service by sending

00:25:04.300 --> 00:25:07.700
lists of important keywords
from the current page describing

00:25:07.700 --> 00:25:08.330
context.

00:25:08.330 --> 00:25:12.230
And there's important things
to note here, just two things.

00:25:12.230 --> 00:25:13.760
In addition to
the name, there is

00:25:13.760 --> 00:25:18.650
metadata that is used to
configure the services

00:25:18.650 --> 00:25:22.100
visibility as cluster local.

00:25:22.100 --> 00:25:24.050
And this means that
there's no public access

00:25:24.050 --> 00:25:24.920
to the add service.

00:25:24.920 --> 00:25:28.490
It's a private cluster
local-only service

00:25:28.490 --> 00:25:30.875
that is accessible from
within the cluster.

00:25:33.480 --> 00:25:36.000
The container port
is set to 8080.

00:25:36.000 --> 00:25:39.060
And although that's
actually the default,

00:25:39.060 --> 00:25:42.582
because we need
to provide this--

00:25:42.582 --> 00:25:44.040
and I'll explain
this in a moment--

00:25:44.040 --> 00:25:45.873
we've got to provide
the full configuration.

00:25:45.873 --> 00:25:48.660
So we do have to
specify container port.

00:25:48.660 --> 00:25:50.460
The reason why
we're providing name

00:25:50.460 --> 00:25:56.270
is this is a specific name
that indicates that we're going

00:25:56.270 --> 00:25:58.360
to be communicating with GRPC.

00:25:58.360 --> 00:26:02.760
Or technically, this
is HTTP/2 over TCP.

00:26:02.760 --> 00:26:05.400
That's what the H2C stands for.

00:26:05.400 --> 00:26:09.090
If this isn't provided, then
the default under Knative

00:26:09.090 --> 00:26:10.500
would be HTTP/1.

00:26:14.022 --> 00:26:14.940
Let's see.

00:26:14.940 --> 00:26:17.910
Moving on, cart service.

00:26:17.910 --> 00:26:20.550
OK, so this provides the
shopping cart functionality

00:26:20.550 --> 00:26:21.780
for the app.

00:26:21.780 --> 00:26:25.950
The configuration should start
looking pretty familiar by now.

00:26:25.950 --> 00:26:28.240
There's only a couple of
things we need to note here.

00:26:28.240 --> 00:26:33.483
The app does take a port
option, this minus p,

00:26:33.483 --> 00:26:35.400
and we get the value
from the port environment

00:26:35.400 --> 00:26:38.700
variable that's set by Knative.

00:26:38.700 --> 00:26:41.550
It's listening on
8080, and, again,

00:26:41.550 --> 00:26:48.100
this is a GRPC service,
HTTP/2 over TCP.

00:26:48.100 --> 00:26:50.670
A couple of other environment
variables getting set here.

00:26:56.000 --> 00:26:57.050
And basically, that's it.

00:26:59.980 --> 00:27:01.535
And let's see, checkout service.

00:27:05.940 --> 00:27:08.820
Checkout service is
used to purchase items

00:27:08.820 --> 00:27:10.680
that the customer has
added to their cart.

00:27:10.680 --> 00:27:16.320
And like the front end and the
cart service that we just saw,

00:27:16.320 --> 00:27:18.390
the checkout service uses
environment variables

00:27:18.390 --> 00:27:21.710
to get the addresses to
use for its back ends

00:27:21.710 --> 00:27:23.250
that it communicates with.

00:27:23.250 --> 00:27:25.290
And that's it here.

00:27:25.290 --> 00:27:28.560
Currency service is used to
convert currencies and display

00:27:28.560 --> 00:27:30.120
price.

00:27:30.120 --> 00:27:34.350
And here, we can see
that it actually listens

00:27:34.350 --> 00:27:36.460
on a specific port, 7000.

00:27:36.460 --> 00:27:39.570
So this would need to
be specified anyway,

00:27:39.570 --> 00:27:43.410
even if we weren't using
GRPC, which we are.

00:27:46.920 --> 00:27:50.220
Email service sends ordering
and payment confirmation

00:27:50.220 --> 00:27:51.900
after purchase.

00:27:51.900 --> 00:27:54.870
And it's already been mentioned,
but the only reason, again,

00:27:54.870 --> 00:27:57.930
for this specific port,
even though it's a default,

00:27:57.930 --> 00:28:00.438
is because, again,
this is a GRPC.

00:28:00.438 --> 00:28:01.980
All of these services
are going to be

00:28:01.980 --> 00:28:03.540
GRPC except for the front end.

00:28:07.228 --> 00:28:10.010
We covered the front end.

00:28:10.010 --> 00:28:13.495
Payment service is used to
make purchases at checkout.

00:28:13.495 --> 00:28:17.170
And there's nothing really
new to explain about this.

00:28:17.170 --> 00:28:22.000
Product catalog service is
used to convert currencies

00:28:22.000 --> 00:28:24.220
when displaying price.

00:28:24.220 --> 00:28:27.130
And again, this
should look familiar.

00:28:27.130 --> 00:28:29.320
Nothing new to explain here.

00:28:29.320 --> 00:28:32.320
Recommendation service is
used to make similar product

00:28:32.320 --> 00:28:34.380
recommendations while
browsing and nothing new

00:28:34.380 --> 00:28:36.280
to explain here.

00:28:36.280 --> 00:28:40.900
And the shipping service, which
is used to purchase products--

00:28:40.900 --> 00:28:43.392
which is used to ship
your purchase products,

00:28:43.392 --> 00:28:45.100
and there's nothing
new to explain there.

00:28:45.100 --> 00:28:48.790
So this is all we had to
do to take our Kubernetes

00:28:48.790 --> 00:28:52.580
manifest-- it was basically
about what we stripped out.

00:28:52.580 --> 00:28:55.600
We did have to add some things.

00:28:55.600 --> 00:29:01.150
We had to add labels that
constrained our visibility

00:29:01.150 --> 00:29:09.580
and had to provide these hints
as to the type of traffic

00:29:09.580 --> 00:29:11.810
that we want.

00:29:11.810 --> 00:29:14.620
And that's it.

00:29:14.620 --> 00:29:18.622
So now let's just take a
look at deploying the app.

00:29:18.622 --> 00:29:20.080
I'll go ahead and
start up a couple

00:29:20.080 --> 00:29:23.410
of watches for our
Kubernetes deployment

00:29:23.410 --> 00:29:26.230
and also around the
case service resources.

00:29:26.230 --> 00:29:29.080
That just gives us
another perspective

00:29:29.080 --> 00:29:31.180
on deploying these services.

00:29:31.180 --> 00:29:39.290
I'll want to deploy
our back end for Redis.

00:29:39.290 --> 00:29:43.480
Remember, this was a state-full
service with a volume attached,

00:29:43.480 --> 00:29:46.490
so this is just a normal
Kubernetes deployment.

00:29:46.490 --> 00:29:48.950
And then I'm going to
deploy the app itself.

00:29:52.760 --> 00:29:55.420
And for now, I'm going to deploy
the second version of the app.

00:29:55.420 --> 00:29:57.400
Go to the repo,
check out the lab

00:29:57.400 --> 00:30:03.310
to see what it means to
have scaling to 0 configured

00:30:03.310 --> 00:30:07.780
and what it means for auto
scaling up in the V3 version.

00:30:07.780 --> 00:30:10.330
But this will just be
the V2 version, where

00:30:10.330 --> 00:30:12.230
I've set minimum scale to 1.

00:30:12.230 --> 00:30:12.730
So

00:30:12.730 --> 00:30:15.400
The services never
actually scale to 0.

00:30:15.400 --> 00:30:17.720
Because for these
services for the website,

00:30:17.720 --> 00:30:21.565
we're going to expect a
responsive user interface.

00:30:21.565 --> 00:30:24.660
So this is a nice
thing to be able to do.

00:30:24.660 --> 00:30:27.070
But scaling to 0 is
normally the default.

00:30:27.070 --> 00:30:29.440
That's also great
for services that

00:30:29.440 --> 00:30:33.820
don't need to run all the time
and consume compute resources

00:30:33.820 --> 00:30:36.220
for what you're getting billed.

00:30:36.220 --> 00:30:42.442
Now that the app is
running, I can curl it.

00:30:42.442 --> 00:30:43.900
So let's see, to
do that, I'm going

00:30:43.900 --> 00:30:45.430
to need to know the gateway IP.

00:30:51.046 --> 00:30:56.660
Send the GKE system
namespace or Istio ingress.

00:30:56.660 --> 00:30:57.980
And there's the IP.

00:30:57.980 --> 00:31:05.230
I'll also need to know the
DNS name for the route--

00:31:05.230 --> 00:31:06.080
for the front end.

00:31:11.340 --> 00:31:14.190
And what we'll see here
is actually, by default,

00:31:14.190 --> 00:31:16.490
the domain would've
been example.com.

00:31:16.490 --> 00:31:21.950
I provided a custom mapping,
just another configuration

00:31:21.950 --> 00:31:23.720
that you apply.

00:31:23.720 --> 00:31:28.820
I took this basic template,
and I configured for a domain

00:31:28.820 --> 00:31:31.010
that I control, tonypujals.com.

00:31:31.010 --> 00:31:33.400
So that's what we're
seeing right there.

00:31:33.400 --> 00:31:35.700
If I curl it, this
request is going

00:31:35.700 --> 00:31:39.500
to go to the ingress
gateway, and it

00:31:39.500 --> 00:31:42.080
needs to know to which
service to send it.

00:31:42.080 --> 00:31:44.150
And so with curl,
I'm going to do that

00:31:44.150 --> 00:31:45.380
by providing a host header.

00:31:50.350 --> 00:31:55.530
So our front-end service
in the default namespace,

00:31:55.530 --> 00:31:57.390
it would, by default,
be example.com.

00:31:57.390 --> 00:31:59.670
But in this case, since I
provided a custom domain

00:31:59.670 --> 00:32:02.370
mapping, it's tonypujals.com.

00:32:02.370 --> 00:32:04.890
And here is the IP that
we're going to send that to.

00:32:09.228 --> 00:32:10.160
And there you go.

00:32:10.160 --> 00:32:12.650
We can see that
a page responded.

00:32:12.650 --> 00:32:15.070
We can also do this
in the browser.

00:32:15.070 --> 00:32:17.310
Now if I send it
to the IP address,

00:32:17.310 --> 00:32:19.380
obviously, I can't
set it to example.com.

00:32:19.380 --> 00:32:22.260
Since that's not my domain, I
can't configure records for it.

00:32:22.260 --> 00:32:24.180
If I set it to the
IP address, I need

00:32:24.180 --> 00:32:25.980
to do something
similar like we just

00:32:25.980 --> 00:32:27.990
did here when we curled it.

00:32:27.990 --> 00:32:32.580
I need to supply
that host header.

00:32:32.580 --> 00:32:35.790
So you can use a mod
header Chrome extension,

00:32:35.790 --> 00:32:39.000
specify your host header,
apply some filter pattern

00:32:39.000 --> 00:32:41.540
so it doesn't apply to every
other website that you go to.

00:32:44.652 --> 00:32:45.360
And there you go.

00:32:45.360 --> 00:32:47.373
This is great for
local testing until you

00:32:47.373 --> 00:32:49.290
go through the trouble
of actually configuring

00:32:49.290 --> 00:32:51.900
that custom domain,
but you can test.

00:32:54.420 --> 00:32:56.010
Since I did configure
for my domain,

00:32:56.010 --> 00:33:00.390
I can also take advantage
of the automatic certificate

00:33:00.390 --> 00:33:02.280
for HTTPS support.

00:33:02.280 --> 00:33:04.830
And there you go, I'm at the
top level of tonypujals.com.

00:33:04.830 --> 00:33:06.270
Here's the application.

00:33:06.270 --> 00:33:07.570
You've never seen it before.

00:33:07.570 --> 00:33:12.720
It's a simple online
shopping experience.

00:33:12.720 --> 00:33:15.395
For each one of these
items, I can drill in.

00:33:15.395 --> 00:33:16.770
I can see that
the recommendation

00:33:16.770 --> 00:33:19.350
service is providing other
products that are similar.

00:33:19.350 --> 00:33:20.220
I can see ads.

00:33:20.220 --> 00:33:24.070
If I refresh this page,
some stuff will update.

00:33:24.070 --> 00:33:29.892
Add stuff to the cart, place
your order, off you go.

00:33:29.892 --> 00:33:31.350
Last thing I want
to you real quick

00:33:31.350 --> 00:33:35.010
is I want to go over
to the Cloud Run

00:33:35.010 --> 00:33:39.755
dashboard in our console.

00:33:39.755 --> 00:33:41.130
There's all the
services running.

00:33:41.130 --> 00:33:42.588
For each one of
these services, you

00:33:42.588 --> 00:33:49.270
